**Things worth discussing once you have numbers**

- Which emotion pairs get confused, and do they get worse with noise?
- Does noise augmentation help on noise types it never saw (e.g. `real` noise)?
- INT8 is not automatically faster. Dynamic quantization adds work at run time, and recurrent (GRU)
  layers are not quantized by this method, so the CRNN may shrink very little. Check the op list in
  `runs/<name>/export_report.json` and compare latency on the target hardware, not only on a laptop.

## Design decisions (and why)

- **Split by actor, not by file.** Clips of the same speaker in train and test leak voice identity
  and inflate scores. Actors 1-16 train, 17-20 validation, 21-24 test (gender-balanced).
- **UAR as the main metric.** Class sizes are not equal (RAVDESS has fewer "neutral" clips), and UAR
  is not dominated by the biggest class.
- **Model selection on validation only.** The test set is used once per model.
- **Same noisy test clips for every model.** Noise is generated with fixed seeds, so FP32, INT8 and
  the baseline are compared on identical inputs.
- **Features in NumPy.** One implementation for training and deployment, and no PyTorch in the
  inference process, which keeps memory measurements honest.
- **Each benchmark in its own process.** Peak memory of one model is not affected by the others.
- **A baseline first.** A neural network is only interesting if it beats MFCC + SVM.

## Limitations

- RAVDESS is **acted** speech by 24 North American actors, in English, recorded in a studio.
  Results will not transfer directly to spontaneous speech, other languages, or real in-car audio.
- The `cabin` noise is a **synthetic approximation**, not a real recording. Use `--noise_dir`
  with real noise for claims about real environments.
- SNR is computed over the whole 3 s clip (including any zero padding).
- Only categorical emotions are predicted. Arousal/valence regression is listed below as future work.
- With 24 speakers, results vary with the actor split and the random seed. Report several seeds
  if you want to claim small differences.

## Ideas for extension

- Pre-trained speech encoders (wav2vec 2.0, WavLM, emotion2vec, or the Whisper encoder) as feature
  extractors, compared on the same noise sweep and edge benchmark.
- Arousal / valence regression with CCC as the metric (needs a dataset such as MSP-Podcast; check its access terms).
- Static INT8 quantization with calibration data, or FP16, and compare to dynamic INT8.
- Cross-corpus test (train on RAVDESS, test on CREMA-D or a Turkish emotional speech corpus).
- Multiple seeds with confidence intervals.

## Project structure

```
src/ser/
  data.py         RAVDESS parsing, actor split, loading
  features.py     log-mel and MFCC statistics (NumPy)
  noise.py        noise types + exact-SNR mixing
  models.py       SmallCNN, CRNN
  train.py        training (optional noise augmentation)
  baseline.py     MFCC + SVM with the same noise sweep
  evaluate.py     clean + noisy evaluation for torch / onnx_fp32 / onnx_int8
  export_onnx.py  ONNX export, parity check, INT8 quantization
  benchmark.py    latency / RTF / memory / size on the current machine
  plots.py        robustness and trade-off figures
  synthetic.py    tiny fake dataset for smoke tests
tests/            pytest (unit + end-to-end)
results/          CSVs and figures produced by the experiments
```

